{
  "skillId": "natural-domain-from-equation",
  "atomType": "R",
  "theory": {
    "intro": "The natural domain of a function is every $x$-value that produces a valid output. Two operations are not allowed: dividing by zero, and taking the square root of a negative number. Find the natural domain by ruling out any $x$ that would break one of these rules, then write the answer as an inequality and in interval notation.",
    "facts": [
      "A denominator can never be zero, so exclude any $x$ that makes a denominator equal to $0$.",
      "A radicand (the number under a square root) must be $\\geq0$.",
      "If a square root sits in a denominator, its radicand must be strictly $>0$, since it also cannot be zero.",
      "A polynomial such as $x^2$ or $3x$ has no denominator or root, so its domain is all real numbers, $(-\\infty,\\infty)$.",
      "State the domain both as an inequality and in interval notation."
    ],
    "steps": [
      "Look for denominators and square roots in the rule.",
      "Set each denominator not equal to zero and solve for the excluded values.",
      "Set each radicand $\\geq0$ (or $>0$ if the root is in a denominator) and solve.",
      "Write the domain as an inequality and in interval notation."
    ]
  },
  "practice": {
